.Sh DESCRIPTION
A collection of utility functions that simplify and add type safety to
the
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
arrays.
The following
.Sy Vt TYPE
names are supported:
.Bl -bullet
.It
double
.It
int
.It
int32 (int32_t)
.It
int64 (int64_t)
.It
long int (long)
.It
time_t
.It
size_t
.It
uint32 (uint32_t)
.It
uint64 (uint64_t)
.It
unsigned int (uint)
.It
unsigned long int (ulong)
.El
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_TYPE
are a series of macros designed to assist initialising an array of
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structures.
Each of these macros defines a parameter of the specified
.Sy Vt TYPE
with the provided
.Fa key
and parameter variable
.Vt address .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_utf8_string ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_octet_string ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_utf8_ptr ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_octet_ptr ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_BN
are macros that provide support for defining UTF8 strings, OCTET strings
and big numbers.
A parameter with name
.Fa key
is defined.
The storage for this parameter is at
.Vt address
and is of
.Em size
bytes.
.Pp
.Dv OSSL_PARAM_END
provides an end of parameter list marker.
This should terminate all
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
arrays.
.Pp
The
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_DEFN
macro provides the ability to construct a single
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
(typically used in the construction of
.Vt OSSL_PARAM
arrays).
The
.Fa key ,
.Em type ,
.Em addr
and
.Em sz
arguments correspond to the
.Fa key ,
.Em data_type ,
.Em data
and
.Em data_size
fields of the
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure as described on the
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
page.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_TYPE
are a series of functions that create
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
records dynamically.
A parameter with name
.Fa key
is created.
The parameter will use storage pointed to by
.Fa buf
and return size of
.Em ret .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_BN
is a function that constructs a large integer
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
A parameter with name
.Fa key ,
storage
.Fa buf ,
size
.Fa bsize
and return size
.Em rsize
is created.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_string
is a function that constructs a UTF8 string
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
A parameter with name
.Fa key ,
storage
.Fa buf
and size
.Fa bsize
is created.
If
.Fa bsize
is zero, the string length is determined using strlen(3). Generally pass
zero for
.Fa bsize
instead of calling strlen(3) yourself.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string
is a function that constructs an OCTET string
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
A parameter with name
.Fa key ,
storage
.Fa buf
and size
.Fa bsize
is created.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_ptr
is a function that constructs a UTF8 string pointer
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
A parameter with name
.Fa key ,
storage pointer
.Em *buf
and size
.Fa bsize
is created.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_ptr
is a function that constructs an OCTET string pointer
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
A parameter with name
.Fa key ,
storage pointer
.Em *buf
and size
.Fa bsize
is created.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_end
is a function that constructs the terminating
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_locate
is a function that searches an
.Fa array
of parameters for the one matching the
.Fa key
name.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_locate_const
behaves exactly like
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_locate
except for the presence of
.Vt const
for the
.Fa array
argument and its return value.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_TYPE
retrieves a value of type
.Sy Vt TYPE
from the parameter
.Fa p .
The value is copied to the address
.Fa val .
Type coercion takes place as discussed in the NOTES section.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_TYPE
stores a value
.Fa val
of type
.Sy Vt TYPE
into the parameter
.Fa p .
If the parameter's
.Em data
field is NULL, then only its
.Em return_size
field will be assigned the size the parameter's
.Em data
buffer should have.
Type coercion takes place as discussed in the NOTES section.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_BN
retrieves a BIGNUM from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p .
The BIGNUM referenced by
.Fa val
is updated and is allocated if
.Em *val
is NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_BN
stores the BIGNUM
.Fa val
into the parameter
.Fa p .
If the parameter's
.Em data
field is NULL, then only its
.Em return_size
field will be assigned the size the parameter's
.Em data
buffer should have.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_utf8_string
retrieves a UTF8 string from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p .
The string is stored into
.Em *val
with a size limit of
.Fa max_len ,
which must be large enough to accommodate a terminating NUL byte,
otherwise this function will fail.
If
.Em *val
is NULL, memory is allocated for the string (including the terminating
NUL byte) and
.Fa max_len
is ignored.
If memory is allocated by this function, it must be freed by the caller.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_utf8_string
sets a UTF8 string from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p
to the value referenced by
.Fa val .
If the parameter's
.Em data
field isn't NULL, its
.Em data_size
must indicate that the buffer is large enough to accommodate the string
that
.Fa val
points at, not including the terminating NUL byte, or this function will
fail.
A terminating NUL byte is added only if the parameter's
.Em data_size
indicates the buffer is longer than the string length, otherwise the
string will not be NUL terminated.
If the parameter's
.Em data
field is NULL, then only its
.Em return_size
field will be assigned the minimum size the parameter's
.Em data
buffer should have to accommodate the string, not including a
terminating NUL byte.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_octet_string
retrieves an OCTET string from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p .
The OCTETs are either stored into
.Em *val
with a length limit of
.Fa max_len
or, in the case when
.Em *val
is NULL, memory is allocated and
.Fa max_len
is ignored.
.Em *used_len
is populated with the number of OCTETs stored.
If
.Fa val
is NULL then the OCTETS are not stored, but
.Em *used_len
is still populated.
If memory is allocated by this function, it must be freed by the caller.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_octet_string
sets an OCTET string from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p
to the value referenced by
.Fa val .
If the parameter's
.Em data
field is NULL, then only its
.Em return_size
field will be assigned the size the parameter's
.Em data
buffer should have.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_utf8_ptr
retrieves the UTF8 string pointer from the parameter referenced by
.Fa p
and stores it in
.Em *val .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_utf8_ptr
sets the UTF8 string pointer in the parameter referenced by
.Fa p
to the values
.Fa val .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_octet_ptr
retrieves the OCTET string pointer from the parameter referenced by
.Fa p
and stores it in
.Em *val .
The length of the OCTET string is stored in
.Em *used_len .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_octet_ptr
sets the OCTET string pointer in the parameter referenced by
.Fa p
to the values
.Fa val .
The length of the OCTET string is provided by
.Fa used_len .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_utf8_string_ptr
retrieves the pointer to a UTF8 string from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p ,
and stores that pointer in
.Em *val .
This is different from
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_utf8_string ,
which copies the string.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_octet_string_ptr
retrieves the pointer to a octet string from the parameter pointed to by
.Fa p ,
and stores that pointer in
.Em *val ,
along with the string's length in
.Em *used_len .
This is different from
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_octet_string ,
which copies the string.
.Pp
The
.Dv OSSL_PARAM_UNMODIFIED
macro is used to detect if a parameter was set.
On creation, via either the macros or construct calls, the
.Em return_size
field is set to this.
If the parameter is set using the calls defined herein, the
.Em return_size
field is changed.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_modified
queries if the parameter
.Fa param
has been set or not using the calls defined herein.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_all_unmodified
resets the unused indicator for all parameters in the array
.Fa params .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_TYPE ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_BN ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_string ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string ,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_ptr
and
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_ptr
return a populated
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_locate
and
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_locate_const
return a pointer to the matching
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
object.
They return NULL on error or when no object matching
.Fa key
exists in the
.Fa array .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_modified
returns 1 if the parameter was set and 0 otherwise.
.Pp
All other functions return 1 on success and 0 on failure.
.Sh NOTES
Native types will be converted as required only if the value is exactly
representable by the target type or parameter.
Apart from that, the functions must be used appropriately for the
expected type of the parameter.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_get_BN
and
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_set_BN
only support nonnegative
.Vt BIGNUM Ns s
when the desired data type is
.Sy OSSL_PARAM_UNSIGNED_INTEGER .
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_BN
currently constructs an
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
structure with the data type
.Sy OSSL_PARAM_UNSIGNED_INTEGER .
.Pp
For
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_ptr
and
.Xr OSSL_PARAM_consstruct_octet_ptr 3 ,
.Fa bsize
is not relevant if the purpose is to send the
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array to a
.Em responder ,
i.e.
to get parameter data back.
In that case,
.Fa bsize
can safely be given zero.
See
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
for further information on the possible purposes.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Reusing the examples from
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
to just show how
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
arrays can be handled using the macros and functions defined herein.
.Ss Example 1
This example is for setting parameters on some object:
.Bd -literal
    #include <openssl/core.h>

    const char *foo = "some string";
    size_t foo_l = strlen(foo);
    const char bar[] = "some other string";
    const OSSL_PARAM set[] = {
        OSSL_PARAM_utf8_ptr("foo", &foo, foo_l),
        OSSL_PARAM_utf8_string("bar", bar, sizeof(bar) - 1),
        OSSL_PARAM_END
    };
.Ed
.Ss Example 2
This example is for requesting parameters on some object, and also
demonstrates that the requester isn't obligated to request all available
parameters:
.Bd -literal
    const char *foo = NULL;
    char bar[1024];
    OSSL_PARAM request[] = {
        OSSL_PARAM_utf8_ptr("foo", &foo, 0),
        OSSL_PARAM_utf8_string("bar", bar, sizeof(bar)),
        OSSL_PARAM_END
    };
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Em responder
that receives this array (as
.Qo Li params Qc
in this example) could fill in the parameters like this:
.Bd -literal
    /* OSSL_PARAM *params */

    OSSL_PARAM *p;

    if ((p = OSSL_PARAM_locate(params, "foo")) != NULL)
        OSSL_PARAM_set_utf8_ptr(p, "foo value");
    if ((p = OSSL_PARAM_locate(params, "bar")) != NULL)
        OSSL_PARAM_set_utf8_string(p, "bar value");
    if ((p = OSSL_PARAM_locate(params, "cookie")) != NULL)
        OSSL_PARAM_set_utf8_ptr(p, "cookie value");
.Ed
.Ss Example 3
This example shows a special case where
.Em -Wincompatible-pointer-types-discards-qualifiers
may be set during compilation.
The value for
.Fa buf
cannot be a
.Em const char *
type string.
An alternative in this case would be to use
.Vt OSSL_PARAM
macro abbreviated calls rather than the specific callers which allows
you to define the sha1 argument as a standard character array
.Pf ( Em char[] Ns ).
.Pp
For example, this code:
.Bd -literal
    OSSL_PARAM params[2];
    params[0] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_string("digest", "SHA1", 0);
    params[1] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_end();
.Ed
.Pp
Can be made compatible with the following version:
.Bd -literal
    char sha1[] = "SHA1"; /* sha1 is defined as char[] in this case */
    OSSL_PARAM params[2];

    params[0] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_string("digest", sha1, 0);
    params[1] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_end();
.Ed
